{"cuisine/tamil-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/tamil-cuisine","n":"Tamil cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["South Indian cuisine","cuisine/south-indian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in India","intro":[["Tamil cuisine is a generic term used to describe culinary practices among Tamil-speaking populations, originating from Southern India and neighboring Sri Lanka. It encompasses several distinct styles of cuisine or cooking repertoires, which can be divided at a basic level into Tamil cuisine of Southeastern India and Sri Lankan Tamil cuisine. The former is a fundamental part of what is now known as South Indian cuisine, with deep connections to other cooking styles of the southern Deccan. The latter is distinct to the Tamil-speaking populations natives to Ceylon, co-formed with other cooking styles unique to Sri Lanka (Sri Lankan cuisine). It shares significant culinary links with Southwestern India and Southeast Asia."],["Meats, along with rice, legumes, and lentils, are popular. Dairy products and tamarind are used to provide sour flavors. On special occasions, traditional Tamil dishes are served in a traditional manner, using banana leaves in place of utensils. After eating, the banana leaves are then used as a secondary food for cattle. A typical breakfast meal consists of idli or dosa with chutney, while lunch includes rice, sambar, curd, kuzhambu, and rasam."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"tamil-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tamil_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Cooking From Heart (Tamil Nadu)","ref":"site/cookingfromheart-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"cookingfromheart.com","note":"Tamil Nadu section of a South Indian vegetarian home cooking blog.","url":"https://cookingfromheart.com/category/tamilnadu-special/","suggested":true},{"name":"Chitra's Food Book","ref":"site/chitrasfoodbook-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"chitrasfoodbook.com","note":"Chennai-based home cook's blog of everyday Tamil vegetarian recipes.","url":"https://chitrasfoodbook.com/?p=5163","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Samaithu Paar (Cook and See)","ref":"cookbook/samaithu-paar-cook-and-see","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"S. Meenakshi Ammal","note":"Classic multi-volume guide to Tamil Brahmin vegetarian cooking, in print since the 1950s.","url":"","suggested":true},{"name":"Annapurni: Heritage Cuisine From Tamil Nadu","ref":"cookbook/annapurni-heritage-cuisine-from-tamil-nadu","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Sabita Radhakrishna","note":"Heritage Tamil recipes and food lore, published by Roli Books.","url":"https://rolibooks.com/?p=12410","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Ashoka Halwa","dish/ashoka-halwa"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Ghee","ingredient/ghee"],["Mung bean","ingredient/mung-bean"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["South Indian cuisine","cuisine/south-indian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Within Tamil cooking","count":1,"links":[["Chettinad cuisine","cuisine/chettinad-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"ingredient/clam":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/clam","n":"Clam","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 4 dishes across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Clam is a common name for several species of bivalve mollusc. The word is often applied only to those that are deemed edible and live as infauna, spending most of their lives halfway buried in the sand of the sea floor or riverbeds. Clams have two shells of equal size connected by two adductor muscles and have a powerful burrowing foot. They live in both freshwater and marine environments; in salt water they prefer to burrow down into the mud. The turbidity of the water required varies with species and location; the greatest diversity of these is in North America."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-clam"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clam"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Clam chowder","dish/clam-chowder"],["Clam dip","dish/clam-dip"]]],["Cuisine of New England","cuisine/cuisine-of-new-england",[["Clam cake","dish/clam-cake"]]],["Portuguese cuisine","cuisine/portuguese-cuisine",[["Amêijoas à Bulhão Pato","dish/ameijoas-a-bulhao-pato"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Clams","ingredient/clams"],["Coriander","ingredient/coriander"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Peppercorn","ingredient/peppercorn"],["White wine","ingredient/white-wine"],["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Salt pork","ingredient/salt-pork"],["Cream cheese","ingredient/cream-cheese"],["Sour cream","ingredient/sour-cream"]]},{"h":"Mentioned in","links":[["Wampanoag foodways","cuisine/wampanoag-foodways"]]}]},"ingredient/bread-crumbs":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/bread-crumbs","n":"Bread crumbs","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 10 dishes across 10 cuisines","intro":[["Breadcrumbs are a culinary ingredient consisting of flour or crumbled bread of varying dryness, sometimes with seasonings added. They are used for a variety of purposes, including breading or crumbing foods before frying (such as breaded cutlets like tonkatsu and schnitzel), topping casseroles, stuffing poultry, thickening stews, and adding inexpensive bulk to soups, meatloaves, and similar foods."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-bread-crumbs"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bread_crumbs"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":10,"groups":[["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine",[["Chicken Kiev","dish/chicken-kiev"],["Pozharsky cutlet","dish/pozharsky-cutlet"]]],["Serbian cuisine","cuisine/serbian-cuisine",[["Karađorđeva šnicla","dish/kara-or-eva-snicla"],["